t across several examples, and it holds up! I was looking for the mathematical relationship and now I think I found it. It moved from 3 to 5, 6 to 8, and even 4 to 6. This is consistent across both vertical and horizontal tracks. It makes sense as an animation frame. A +2 represents a single step along the track, which matches the track's spacing. Now I need to see how a test input move from col 8 to col 10!\n\n\n**Predicting The Transformation**\n\nI've nailed down the +2 shift as the core rule. Now, with the test input, I'm predicting that moving from column 8 means the track dot will shift to column 10, meaning a change in the central column. Specifically, at col 10 the block is centered, and cols 9 and 11 will be adjacent. I've rechecked the logic to see how it affects the adjacent cells. I am checking the rows in the output. I am confident I can handle any input now.\n\n\n\n**Constructing The Grid**\n\nI'm now building the output grid! Based on the +2 shift, I've confirmed column 10, with adjacent columns 9 and 11, match the pattern of Example 3. I've re-examined Example 3's output. I'm focusing on the row where the block appears, to determine how the shift affects the values around the central track values. I'm now certain the output is a direct translation!\n\n\n",
